-\abstract{\textbf{Introduction:} Online adaptation of a photovoltaic current predictor does not necessarily increase the energy extracted by a maximum power point tracking controller. This study examines that distinction in firmware targeting an ESP32.\\
-\textbf{Materials and methods:} A sensing-aware digital photovoltaic model was combined with a 2--16--1 neural current predictor and bounded updates of its output layer. Frozen and adaptive networks were compared with perturb and observe, incremental conductance, and adaptive perturb and observe. Seven programmed profiles and ten paired noise seeds produced 350 host simulations. A separate pair of Wokwi runs assessed static operation at 600 W/m$^2$ and 37 $^\circ$C.\\
+\abstract{\textbf{Introduction:} A neural maximum power point tracking algorithm combines current-characteristic identification, bounded online updates and load regulation in firmware targeting an ESP32. Its evaluation distinguishes prediction accuracy from energy tracking.\\
+\textbf{Materials and methods:} The algorithm uses a 2--16--1 current predictor, a two-stage search for the voltage reference and a bounded load correction. Its output layer is updated from measured current, with incremental conductance used outside the covered environmental domain. Frozen and adaptive networks were compared with perturb and observe, incremental conductance, and adaptive perturb and observe. Seven programmed profiles and ten paired noise seeds produced 350 host simulations. A separate pair of Wokwi runs assessed static operation at 600 W/m$^2$ and 37 $^\circ$C.\\
 \textbf{Results:} Across five primary host profiles, mean model-referenced tracking efficiency was 99.429\% for the frozen network and 99.427\% with online updates. In the Wokwi static pair, ten updates occurred with learning enabled. Current prediction root mean square error during the final 30 s was 0.0135 A, compared with 0.1483 A without updates. Recorded energy over a common 120 s window was 26,547.51 and 26,548.38 J, respectively.\\
 \textbf{Conclusions:} The available runs show improved local current prediction without increased energy capture. Dynamic Wokwi comparisons remain in progress. The findings concern a digital plant and simulated firmware execution, not a physical photovoltaic converter.}
