 \section{Discussion}
-The static pair distinguishes local identification from energy tracking. Output-layer updates substantially reduce current residuals, yet the extracted energy remains almost unchanged. A controller can tolerate prediction errors that have little effect on the maximizing voltage. Conversely, a correction that reduces local current error can shift the reference without improving the position of the true maximum.
+The proposed algorithm forms a chain from a measured current residual to parameter correction, reference calculation and load regulation. The static pair shows why each stage must be evaluated separately. It distinguishes local identification from energy tracking. Output-layer updates substantially reduce current residuals, yet the extracted energy remains almost unchanged. In this implementation the learning objective is current approximation, while the reference depends on the maximum of the resulting power curve. The online update does not directly minimize voltage-reference error or maximize measured energy. A controller can therefore tolerate prediction errors that have little effect on the maximizing voltage. Conversely, a correction that reduces local current error can shift the reference without improving the position of the true maximum.
 
 The source structure also explains why changing irradiance need not change the optimal voltage. At fixed temperature, irradiance multiplies current without changing the normalized curve shape. That factor cancels when maximizing predicted power. A nearly constant voltage under changing irradiance is therefore compatible with this plant, but it does not independently demonstrate learning. The conclusion follows from the implementation and should not be generalized to every commercial photovoltaic characteristic.
 
